  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' covers the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-1b08b3e3 Web logs' (id `bf7c3332-8c2f-4b81-95bf-f22c84e2e4fd`) has two panels. One is a line chart of requests over time. The other is a bar chart of requests by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-1b08b3e3 5xx errors' (id `586b4c5d-91dc-4741-abb9-5a03f9c67fed`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive in 5 minutes. The range is on `response.keyword`, so it covers every code in that span. The rule was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n4. **Edits and read-back:** I renamed the response-code panel to 'Responses by code'. I also raised its terms limit from the default 5 to 20 so that every code shows. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`.\n\nI haven't confirmed that the rule has actually executed. Its status was still pending when it was created.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
